**Steps to reproduce or a small repository showing the problem:**

Create some entity with non-empty constructor. For example:

```ts
@Entity()
export class Post {

    @PrimaryGeneratedColumn()
    id: number;

    @Column()
    private title: string;

    constructor(title: string) {
        if (title === undefined) {
            throw new Error("Invalid title value");
        }

        this.title = title;
    }

}
```

When entity is loaded from db, `Invalid title value` is thrown, because constructor is invoked without args. I think calling constructor must be skipped somehow in this case.

comments:
I think just checking the value instead of throwing an error would be a straightforward solution while maintaining the current functionality.


```typescript
@Entity()
export class Post {

    @PrimaryGeneratedColumn()
    id: number;

    @Column()
    private title: string;

    constructor(title?: string) {
        if (title !== undefined) {
            this.title = title;
        }
    }

}
```

This is probably due to the _strictPropertyInitialization_ compile flag, [this](https://www.ryadel.com/en/ts2564-ts-property-has-no-initializer-typescript-error-fix-visual-studio-2017-vs2017/) could help.

You could assign title to a default value if not given, or use the definite assignment assertion modifier(!) like this.

```ts
@Entity()
export class Post {

    @PrimaryGeneratedColumn()
    id: number;

    @Column()
    private title!: string;

    constructor(title?: string) {
        if (title !== undefined) {
            this.title = title;
        }
    }

}
```

I have an example of you can continue to use `strictPropertyInitialization` but unfortunately it's sorta blocked by this other issue with `migration:generate`: https://github.com/typeorm/typeorm/issues/3835#issuecomment-514787130

I suppose that if you don't use `migration:generate` then you could still use this solution:

```typescript
import {
    Entity,
    PrimaryColumn
} from "typeorm";

@Entity()
export class EntityThatCantBeGenerated  {

    @PrimaryColumn("varchar")
    public readonly id: string;

    constructor(input : { id : string }){
        this.id = input.id;
    }
}
```

As a temporary workaround (because I'm hoping this issue get's a PR that fixes the requirement of having a default constructor), you can make a private constructor and then create a static factory function instead. This allows you to have strict object initialization without the side-effect of breaking migrations (as described in https://github.com/typeorm/typeorm/issues/3835#issuecomment-514787130). So here's the temporary workaround:
```typescript
import {
    Entity,
    PrimaryColumn
} from "typeorm";

interface IJustData {
    id: string;
}

@Entity()
export class EntityThatCantBeGenerated implements IJustData   {

    @PrimaryColumn("varchar")
    public readonly id: string;

     /**
     * please use the static ".make" function instead. Read more here: https://github.com/typeorm/typeorm/issues/1772#issuecomment-514787854
     */
    private constructor(){} // <-- notice the private accessor

    public static make (input: IJustData){
         return Object.assign(new EntityThatCantBeGenerated(), input)
}
}
```

The cool part is that TypeOrm is still capable of using the private constructor for things like migrations, but developers won't be able to use the unsafe default constructor. So essentially you get the advantages of `strictPropertyInitialization`.

